   "pages": [
    {
     "page": 1,
     "text": "**CLINICAL SUMMARY - Simmons Williams and Long Medical Group**\n\n**Patient:** Alexander Day \n**DOB:** \n**Sex:** M \n**MRN:** 809830\n\n---\n\n**Encounters:**\n\n- **2024-06-05** \n **Reason for Visit:** Asthma \n **BP:** 118/63 \n **Weight:** 178 lbs\n\n- **2023-08-29** \n **Reason for Visit:** Asthma \n **BP:** 123/70 \n **Weight:** 171 lbs\n\n- **2022-11-21** \n **Reason for Visit:** Asthma \n **BP:** 109/71 \n **Weight:** 171 lbs\n\n- **2022-02-12** \n **Reason for Visit:** Asthma \n **BP:** 115/67 \n **Weight:** 176 lbs\n\n---\n\n**Assessment/Plan:**\n\n- **Asthma** \n - **Current Status:** The patient's asthma is well-controlled.\n - **ER Visit:** There was an emergency room visit approximately 0.3 years ago, indicating a previous exacerbation.\n - **Medication:** The patient is currently taking albuterol 90 mcg as needed for symptom management.\n\n---\n\n**Social History:**\n\n- **Tobacco:** The patient used to smoke cigarettes, with a history of approximately 10 cigarettes per day. He quit smoking in June 2024.\n- **Alcohol:** The patient consumes around 10 alcoholic drinks per week.\n- **Family History:** There is a notable family history of Alzheimer's disease, with the patient's father being diagnosed at the age of 68. The father's current status is deceased.\n\n---\n\n**Clinical Notes:**\n\n**2024-06-05 Visit:**\n\nDuring the visit on June 5, 2024, Mr. Day reported feeling generally well-controlled with his asthma symptoms. He had no recent episodes of wheezing or shortness of breath. The patient's vital signs were stable with a blood pressure of 118/63 and a weight of 178 pounds. His physical examination revealed normal lung sounds, and there were no signs of respiratory distress. Mr. Day was educated on the importance of continuing his medication regimen and avoiding known asthma triggers.\n\n**2023-08-29 Visit:**\n\nOn August 29, 2023, Mr. Day presented with mild asthma symptoms, which were managed effectively with his current treatment plan. His blood pressure was and his weight was 171 pounds. During the physical examination, his lung sounds were clear, and there were no adventitious sounds. He was advised to maintain his medication schedule and attend regular follow-up appointments.\n\n**2022-11-21 Visit:**\n\nDuring the November 21, 2022, visit, Mr. Day reported stable asthma with no recent exacerbations. His vital signs included a blood pressure of 109/71 and a weight of 171 pounds. The physical examination showed clear lung fields with no abnormal breath sounds. He was counseled on adherence to his albuterol inhaler and the importance of av"
    },
    {
     "page": 2,
     "text": "oiding smoking and other irritants.\n\n**2022-02-12 Visit:**\n\nIn the visit on February 12, 2022, Mr. Day presented with mild asthma symptoms. His blood pressure was 115/67, and his weight was 176 pounds. The physical examination demonstrated normal respiratory function without any signs of distress. He was educated on the proper use of his inhaler and the need to monitor for any changes in his symptoms.\n\n---\n\n**Additional Notes:**\n\nMr. Day has a history of smoking, having smoked approximately 10 cigarettes per day starting from January 16, 2014. He successfully quit smoking in June 2024. His alcohol consumption is moderate, with approximately 10 drinks per week. There are no additional reported medical conditions beyond the well-managed asthma. The patient's family history includes a father who was diagnosed with Alzheimer's disease at the age of 68.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nMr. Alexander Day's asthma is well-controlled with appropriate medication and lifestyle modifications. His recent includes stable management of asthma symptoms, with a notable absence of emergency room visits or hospitalizations. The patient is compliant with his treatment regimen and has made positive lifestyle changes by quitting smoking. His overall health status is stable, and he is advised to continue with regular follow-ups to monitor his asthma and address any potential issues promptly.\n\n---\n\n**End of Clinical Summary**\n"
    }
